    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Proper, convex, and lower-semicontinuous extended-real functions on Euclidean space",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$. Write $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ with its usual order and extended addition; in a convex combination set $0\\cdot(+\\infty)=0$ and $\\lambda\\cdot(+\\infty)=+\\infty$ for $\\lambda>0$. A function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ is **proper** when it never takes the value $-\\infty$ and its effective domain\n\n$$\\operatorname{dom}f:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:f(x)<+\\infty\\}$$\n\nis nonempty. It is **convex** when\n\n$$f((1-t)x+ty)\\le (1-t)f(x)+tf(y)\\qquad(x,y\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\ t\\in[0,1]).$$\n\nIt is **lower semicontinuous** when each weak sublevel set $\\{x:f(x)\\le a\\}$ is closed in the Euclidean topology, for every $a\\in\\mathbb R$. Its **epigraph** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{epi}f:=\\{(x,r)\\in\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R:f(x)\\le r\\}.$$\n\nOn this page “closed proper convex” means proper, convex, and lower semicontinuous in these conventions. The pairing of $p,x\\in\\mathbb R^d$ is the Euclidean inner product $\\langle p,x\\rangle$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, Princeton Mathematical Series 28, §§7 and 12",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §§1.2 and 1.4",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Fenchel conjugate, biconjugate, and subdifferential",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be proper in the sense of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Its **Fenchel conjugate** is $f^*:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$,\n\n$$f^*(p):=\\sup_{x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f}\\bigl(\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)\\bigr).$$\n\nThe supremum is taken in $\\overline{\\mathbb R}$; its defining set is nonempty and consists of real numbers, so $f^*(p)\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$. The **biconjugate** is an extended-real expression that may also take $-\\infty$ when its defining supremum is over the empty set:\n\n$$f^{**}(x):=\\sup_{\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^d:f^*(p)<+\\infty\\}}\\bigl(\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\bigr),$$\n\nwith the convention $\\sup\\varnothing=-\\infty$. For $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, the **subdifferential** is\n\n$$\\partial f(x):=\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^d:f(y)\\ge f(x)+\\langle p,y-x\\rangle\\text{ for every }y\\in\\mathbb R^d\\};$$\n\nset $\\partial f(x)=\\varnothing$ for $x\\notin\\operatorname{dom}f$. A vector in $\\partial f(x)$ is a subgradient at $x$. All pairings use [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §12 and §23",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §§2.1 and 2.3",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Zhiyuan Li, Lecture 4: Convex Conjugates and Marginal Duality",
              "url": "https://zhiyuanli.ttic.edu/convex-optimization-site/Lecture-4___-Convex-Conjugates___-and-Marginal-Duality/"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Extended-real convexity and lower semicontinuity are characterized by the epigraph",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-metric-topology",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ use the conventions of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Then $f$ is convex if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is a convex subset of $\\mathbb R^{d+1}$, and $f$ is lower semicontinuous if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is closed.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Suppose $f$ is convex and take $(x,r),(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. For $t\\in[0,1]$, the chord inequality gives $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)\\le(1-t)r+ts$, so $(1-t)(x,r)+t(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. Thus the epigraph is convex. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n1.2 Conversely, if the epigraph is convex and $f(x),f(y)$ are finite, then $(x,f(x))$ and $(y,f(y))$ lie in it; their convex combination lies in it and gives the chord inequality. If either value is $+\\infty$ and its coefficient is positive, the right side is $+\\infty$ and the inequality is automatic; at $t=0$ or $t=1$ it is equality. [F1, algebra]\n\n1.3 If the epigraph is closed, for each $a\\in\\mathbb R$ the map $x\\mapsto(x,a)$ is continuous and $\\{x:f(x)\\le a\\}$ is its preimage of $\\operatorname{epi}f$, hence is closed. [F1, F2, construct]\n\n2.1 Conversely suppose every weak sublevel set is closed and let $(x,r)\\notin\\operatorname{epi}f$, so $r<f(x)$. Choose a finite real $a$ with $r<a<f(x)$ when $f(x)$ is finite, and choose $a=r+1$ when $f(x)=+\\infty$. The set $N=\\{y:f(y)>a\\}$ is an open neighborhood of $x$, as it is the complement of a closed weak sublevel set. Choose $\\eta>0$ with $r+\\eta<a$. For every $y\\in N$ and $s\\in(r-\\eta,r+\\eta)$ one has $s<a<f(y)$, so $(y,s)\\notin\\operatorname{epi}f$. Thus $N\\times(r-\\eta,r+\\eta)$ is an open neighborhood of $(x,r)$ disjoint from the epigraph. Its complement is open at every point outside the epigraph, so the epigraph is closed. [given, F1, F2, construct] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §§7 and 12",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §§1.2 and 1.4",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Strictly separate a point from a nonempty closed convex Euclidean set",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $z\\notin E$. Then there are a nonzero $u\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and a real $c$ such that\n\n$$\\langle u,e\\rangle\\le c<\\langle u,z\\rangle\\qquad(e\\in E).$$\n\nThus an affine hyperplane strictly separates the point from the entire set.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Choose $e_0\\in E$ and set $R=\\|z-e_0\\|_2+1$. The set $K=E\\cap\\{e:\\|z-e\\|_2\\le R\\}$ is nonempty and bounded; it is closed because $E$ is closed and the distance map is continuous by [L1]. Hence [L2] makes it compact, and the continuous function $e\\mapsto\\|z-e\\|_2^2$ attains a minimum at some $e_*\\in K$ by [L3]. [given, L1, L2, L3, choose]\n\n2.1 This $e_*$ is nearest to $z$ in all of $E$: every $e\\in E\\setminus K$ satisfies $\\|z-e\\|_2>R>\\|z-e_0\\|_2\\ge\\|z-e_*\\|_2$. Put $u=z-e_*$; since $z\\notin E$, $u\\ne0$. [step 1.1, given, algebra]\n\n3.1 Fix $e\\in E$ and put $v=e-e_*$. For $0<t\\le1$, [F2] gives $e_*+tv\\in E$, so minimality of $e_*$ and [F1] imply $2t\\langle u,v\\rangle\\le t^2\\|v\\|_2^2$. If $\\langle u,v\\rangle>0$, then $v\\ne0$; setting $t=\\min\\{1,\\langle u,v\\rangle/\\|v\\|_2^2\\}$ contradicts this inequality whether the minimum is $1$ or the quotient. Hence $\\langle u,v\\rangle\\le0$. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 Therefore $\\langle u,e\\rangle\\le\\langle u,e_*\\rangle$ for every $e\\in E$, while $\\langle u,z\\rangle=\\langle u,e_*\\rangle+\\|u\\|_2^2>\\langle u,e_*\\rangle$. Taking $c=\\langle u,e_*\\rangle$ proves strict separation. [F1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §11, especially Theorem 11.5",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §1.1.1",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Fenchel inequality and its equality condition",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be proper, let $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, and let $p\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Then $f^*(p)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$. Equality holds exactly when $p\\in\\partial f(x)$, equivalently when $x$ attains the supremum defining $f^*(p)$; in that case the supremum is finite.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Since $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, the value $\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$ is one of the values over which $f^*(p)$ is the supremum; the stated inequality follows. [F1, given]\n\n2.1 Equality means every $y\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ satisfies $\\langle p,y\\rangle-f(y)\\le\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$, which rearranges to the subgradient inequality; for $y\\notin\\operatorname{dom}f$ that inequality is automatic because $f(y)=+\\infty$. Conversely the subgradient inequalities make the value at $x$ a finite upper bound attained there, so $f^*(p)=\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$. [F1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §23, Theorem 23.5",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §2.3",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Zhiyuan Li, Lecture 4: Convex Conjugates and Marginal Duality",
              "url": "https://zhiyuanli.ttic.edu/convex-optimization-site/Lecture-4___-Convex-Conjugates___-and-Marginal-Duality/"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite-dimensional Fenchel-Moreau biconjugation",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
          "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
          "thm-of-archimedean"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-of-archimedean": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be closed, proper, and convex in the sense of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. With the Fenchel conjugate and biconjugate of [[def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient]], one has\n\n$$f^{**}(x)=f(x)\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb R^d).$$",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 For any $x$ with $f(x)<+\\infty$ and any $p$ with $f^*(p)<+\\infty$, the defining supremum gives $f^*(p)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$, hence $\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\le f(x)$; taking the supremum over $p$ yields $f^{**}(x)\\le f(x)$. If $f(x)=+\\infty$, the same upper bound is automatic. [F4, given, algebra]\n\n1.2 Fix $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ and a real $t<f(x)$. Apply [F3] to $(x,t)$ and the nonempty closed convex set $\\operatorname{epi}f$, and negate the resulting separator to write $\\langle a,y\\rangle+bs\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle+bt$ for all $(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. Upward closure forces $b\\ge0$. If $b=0$, the inequality at $(x,f(x))\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$ would give $\\langle a,x\\rangle\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle$, impossible. Thus $b>0$. [F2, F3, given, algebra]\n\n1.3 Now fix $x\\notin\\operatorname{dom}f$ and $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Apply [F3] to $(x,t)$ and negate the separator to obtain $\\langle a,y\\rangle+bs\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle+bt$ for all $(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. If $b>0$, then $f(y)\\ge-\\langle a/b,y\\rangle+c/b$ for every $y\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, so with $p=-a/b$ one has $f^*(p)\\le-c/b<+\\infty$ and $f^{**}(x)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\ge(c-\\langle a,x\\rangle)/b>t$. [F2, F3, F4, algebra]\n\n2.1 Substituting $(y,f(y))$ for every $y\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ gives the affine minorant $\\ell(y)=-\\langle a/b,y\\rangle+c/b\\le f(y)$. With $p=-a/b$, [F4] gives $f^*(p)\\le-c/b$, hence $f^{**}(x)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\ge\\ell(x)>t$. Since this holds for every $t<f(x)$, $f^{**}(x)\\ge f(x)$ on $\\operatorname{dom}f$. [F4, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n2.2 In the $b=0$ case of step 1.3, the separator gives $\\langle a,y\\rangle\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle$ for every $y\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$. Choose $y_0\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ and a finite $t_0<f(y_0)$. Apply [F3] to $(y_0,t_0)$ and $\\operatorname{epi}f$, reversing the separator orientation to obtain $\\langle a_0,y\\rangle+b_0s\\ge c_0>\\langle a_0,y_0\\rangle+b_0t_0$ for every $(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. Upward closure gives $b_0\\ge0$, and $b_0=0$ contradicts the inequality at $(y_0,f(y_0))$, so $b_0>0$. Set $p_0=-a_0/b_0$ and $r_0=c_0/b_0$; substituting $(y,f(y))$ gives the affine minorant $\\ell_0(y)=\\langle p_0,y\\rangle+r_0\\le f(y)$ on $\\operatorname{dom}f$, so [F4] gives $f^*(p_0)\\le-r_0$. For each positive integer $k$, define $\\ell_k(y)=\\ell_0(y)+k(c-\\langle a,y\\rangle)=\\langle p_0-ka,y\\rangle+(r_0+kc)$. Since $c-\\langle a,y\\rangle\\le0$ on $\\operatorname{dom}f$, this is still a minorant; [F4] gives $f^*(p_0-ka)\\le-(r_0+kc)<+\\infty$ and hence $f^{**}(x)\\ge\\ell_k(x)=\\ell_0(x)+k(c-\\langle a,x\\rangle)$. The coefficient $c-\\langle a,x\\rangle$ is positive by step 1.3, so [L1] makes these lower bounds exceed every prescribed real number as $k$ ranges over the positive integers. Thus $f^{**}(x)=+\\infty=f(x)$. [F1, F2, F3, F4, L1, step 1.3, choose, algebra]\n\n3.1 The upper bound from step 1.1 and the lower bounds from steps 2.1 and 2.2 cover respectively finite and infinite values of $f(x)$, proving equality everywhere. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §12, Theorems 12.1 and 12.2",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §2.2, Theorem 2.5",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "D. P. Bertsekas, A. Nedić, and A. E. Ozdaglar, MIT 6.253 complete lecture notes, Lecture 7",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-253-convex-analysis-and-optimization-spring-2012/6c63c6219c60378bc27d5b4a9167f1bc_MIT6_253S12_lec_comp.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Zhiyuan Li, Lecture 4: Convex Conjugates and Marginal Duality, Theorem 4.3",
              "url": "https://zhiyuanli.ttic.edu/convex-optimization-site/Lecture-4___-Convex-Conjugates___-and-Marginal-Duality/"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-fenchel-attainment",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A coercive Fenchel tilt attains its minimum and conjugate supremum",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
