          "lem-finite-set-has-max": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-inf-epsilon": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-compact-iff-finite-intersection-property": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-infimum-property": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be closed, proper, and convex, let $p\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and define $g(x)=f(x)-\\langle p,x\\rangle$. Assume $g$ is **coercive**, meaning that for every $M\\in\\mathbb R$ there is $R>0$ such that $\\|x\\|_2\\ge R$ implies $g(x)>M$. Then $g$ has a finite global minimum at some $x_*\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$. The supremum defining $f^*(p)$ is finite and attained at $x_*$, and $p\\in\\partial f(x_*)$. No uniqueness is asserted.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Since $f$ is proper, choose $x_0\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ and put $c=g(x_0)\\in\\mathbb R$. Coercivity supplies $R>\\|x_0\\|_2$ such that $g(x)>c+1$ whenever $\\|x\\|_2\\ge R$. Let $K=\\{x:\\|x\\|_2\\le R\\}$; it is nonempty, closed, bounded, and therefore compact by [L3]. [given, L3, choose]\n\n1.2 The epigraph of $g$ is the preimage of $\\operatorname{epi}f$ under the continuous shear in [L2], because $g(x)\\le r$ exactly when $f(x)\\le r+\\langle p,x\\rangle$. Hence $g$ is lower semicontinuous by [L1]. [L1, L2, given, algebra]\n\n2.1 For each real $r>0$, $U_r=\\{x\\in K:g(x)>-r\\}$ is relatively open in $K$, since its complement is the weak sublevel $K\\cap\\{g\\le-r\\}$. These sets cover $K$ because $g$ never equals $-\\infty$; compactness gives a finite subcover, and [L6] supplies a largest real index $r_*$ among its finitely many indices. Since the $U_r$ are nested, $U_{r_*}=K$, so $g$ is bounded below on $K$. [L1, L3, L6, given, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The nonempty set $g[K]$ is bounded below by step 2.1 and has a real infimum $m$. For every $\\varepsilon>0$, the set $F_\\varepsilon=K\\cap\\{x:g(x)\\le m+\\varepsilon\\}$ is closed by [L1] and nonempty by [L5]. Any finite subfamily has nonempty intersection, because the member with least positive $\\varepsilon$ is contained in all the others. [L1, L4, L5, step 1.1, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 Applying [L4] to the family $(F_\\varepsilon)_{\\varepsilon>0}$ gives $x_*\\in\\bigcap_{\\varepsilon>0}F_\\varepsilon$. Since $g(x_*)\\le m+\\varepsilon$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$ and $m$ is a lower bound, $g(x_*)=m$. [L4, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 For $x\\notin K$, step 1.1 gives $g(x)>c+1\\ge m+1$, because $x_0\\in K$ implies $m\\le c$. Thus $x_*$ is a global minimizer over $\\mathbb R^d$, and its value $m$ is finite. [step 1.1, step 4.1, algebra]\n\n6.1 By the definition of $f^*$, $f^*(p)=\\sup_x(-g(x))=-m$, and the supremum is attained at $x_*$. Therefore $f(x_*)+f^*(p)=\\langle p,x_*\\rangle$; [L7] yields $p\\in\\partial f(x_*)$. [given, L7, step 5.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §§12, 23, and 27",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §§1.5 and 2.3",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
