      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-finite-mixture-envelope",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite-mixture convex envelope and its supporting-face condition",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
          "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
          "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality",
          "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $q_1,\\ldots,q_m\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\in\\mathbb R$. Put\n\n$$\\Delta_m=\\{\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^m:\\theta_i\\ge0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i=1\\},\\qquad P:=\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_iq_i:\\theta\\in\\Delta_m\\right\\}.$$\n\nThus $P$ is the finite convex hull of the listed points, defined here as their convex combinations.\n\nFor $x\\in P$ let $\\Theta_x=\\{\\theta\\in\\Delta_m:\\sum_i\\theta_iq_i=x\\}$ and define\n\n$$f_{\\rm mix}(x)=\\min_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_x}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i a_i;$$\n\nset $f_{\\rm mix}(x)=+\\infty$ for $x\\notin P$. Then every minimum is attained; $f_{\\rm mix}$ is proper, lower semicontinuous, and convex; and it is pointwise greatest among convex functions $h:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ satisfying $h(q_i)\\le a_i$ for every $i$. Consequently $f_{\\rm mix}=f_{\\rm mix}^{**}$. If $p\\in\\partial f_{\\rm mix}(x)$ and $\\theta$ is any minimizing mixture for $x$, then every $i$ with $\\theta_i>0$ satisfies\n\n$$a_i=\\langle p,q_i\\rangle-f_{\\rm mix}^*(p).$$\n\nThus all occupied data points $(q_i,a_i)$ lie on one supporting affine hyperplane. The assertion is finite-dimensional and makes no claim about infinite families.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 The simplex $\\Delta_m$ is nonempty (it contains each coordinate vector), closed, and bounded. For $x\\in P$, $\\Theta_x$ is nonempty by the definition of convex hull and is closed and bounded in $\\mathbb R^m$, so [F1] makes it compact. The continuous linear objective $\\theta\\mapsto\\sum_i\\theta_i a_i$ attains its minimum on $\\Theta_x$ by [F3]. [F1, F3, given, construct]\n\n2.1 Let $C=\\{\\sum_i\\theta_i(q_i,a_i):\\theta\\in\\Delta_m\\}\\subset\\mathbb R^{d+1}$ and $V=\\{(0,s):s\\ge0\\}$. The affine map defining $C$ is continuous by [L1], so $C$ is compact by [F2]; it is convex, and $V$ is closed and convex. If $(x,r)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f_{\\rm mix}$, a minimizing $\\theta\\in\\Theta_x$ gives $(x,f_{\\rm mix}(x))\\in C$ and $r-f_{\\rm mix}(x)\\ge0$, so $(x,r)\\in C+V$. Conversely if $(x,r)=\\sum_i\\theta_i(q_i,a_i)+(0,s)$ with $s\\ge0$, then $f_{\\rm mix}(x)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i a_i\\le r$. Thus $\\operatorname{epi}f_{\\rm mix}=C+V$. [F1, F2, L1, step 1.1, construct]\n\n2.2 The coordinate vector with $\\theta_i=1$ and all other weights zero shows $f_{\\rm mix}(q_i)\\le a_i$. Let $h:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be convex with $h(q_i)\\le a_i$. For any weights $\\theta\\in\\Delta_m$, prove $h(\\sum_i\\theta_iq_i)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i h(q_i)$ by induction on the number $N$ of positive weights: for $N=1$ it is equality; for $N>1$, choose an index $j$ with $0<\\theta_j<1$, put $y=(1-\\theta_j)^{-1}\\sum_{i\\ne j}\\theta_iq_i$, and apply the induction hypothesis to the normalized remaining weights. Convexity then gives $h(\\sum_i\\theta_iq_i)\\le\\theta_jh(q_j)+(1-\\theta_j)h(y)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i h(q_i)$. All terms on the right are finite because $h(q_i)\\le a_i$ and the codomain excludes $-\\infty$. For $x\\in P$ and any $\\theta\\in\\Theta_x$, this gives $h(x)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i a_i$; taking the minimum yields $h(x)\\le f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. For $x\\notin P$, $h(x)\\le+\\infty=f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. Thus $f_{\\rm mix}$ is the greatest convex minorant of the data. [F4, given, step 1.1, algebra, induction]\n\n3.1 The set $C+V$ is convex: for $c_1,c_2\\in C$, $v_1,v_2\\in V$, and $t\\in[0,1]$, $(1-t)(c_1+v_1)+t(c_2+v_2)=((1-t)c_1+tc_2)+((1-t)v_1+tv_2)\\in C+V$ by [F5]. To see it is closed, fix $z=(x,r)\\notin C+V$ and write $c=(u,a)\\in C$. The distance from $z$ to the ray $c+V$ is $\\rho_z(c)=\\sqrt{\\|x-u\\|_2^2+\\max\\{a-r,0\\}^2}$: if $r\\ge a$ the closest ray point has height $r$, and if $r<a$ it has height $a$. This formula is continuous in $c$ because $\\max\\{w,0\\}=(w+|w|)/2$, and it is positive for every $c\\in C$ since $z\\notin c+V$. Compactness of $C$ and [F3] give a minimum $\\delta>0$; every point of $C+V$ is at distance at least $\\delta$ from $z$, so the ball of radius $\\delta/2$ about $z$ misses $C+V$. [F1, F2, F3, F5, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 By [F4] and step 3.1, $f_{\\rm mix}$ is convex and lower semicontinuous. It is proper because $P$ is nonempty, each value on $P$ is finite by step 1.1, and it is $+\\infty$ outside $P$. Therefore [F6] gives $f_{\\rm mix}=f_{\\rm mix}^{**}$. [F4, F6, step 1.1, step 3.1]\n\n5.1 Let $p\\in\\partial f_{\\rm mix}(x)$ and let $\\theta$ attain the minimum defining $f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. By [F7], $f_{\\rm mix}^*(p)=\\langle p,x\\rangle-f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. Also $f_{\\rm mix}(q_i)\\le a_i$ implies $\\langle p,q_i\\rangle-a_i\\le\\langle p,q_i\\rangle-f_{\\rm mix}(q_i)\\le f_{\\rm mix}^*(p)$. Since $\\langle p,x\\rangle-f_{\\rm mix}(x)=\\sum_i\\theta_i(\\langle p,q_i\\rangle-a_i)$ is a weighted average of terms bounded above by $f_{\\rm mix}^*(p)$, each term with $\\theta_i>0$ equals that bound, proving the supporting-hyperplane formula. [F7, step 1.1, step 2.2, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §§12, 18, and 23",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
