      {
        "id": "cor-tdmath-ordered-finite-mixture-envelope",
        "kind": "corollary",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Conditional finite-mixture envelope for an additive ordered representation",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Conditional proof complete; upstream batch-3 unit-point-mass notation, zero-mass proof case, and prerequisite-page closure remain escalated.",
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound",
          "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability",
          "lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined",
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "thm-tdmath-finite-mixture-envelope"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-finite-mixture-envelope": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the strict-reference representation conclusion of the draft supplier [[thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation]] for its nonempty state set $X$ and stated comparison hypotheses. For a finite nonempty list of distinct states $s_1,\\ldots,s_m\\in X$, let $e_s$ be the unit-state compound defined in [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]; it is a mass-one point mass by [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]]. For $\\theta\\in\\Delta_m$, put $M_\\theta=\\sum_i\\theta_i e_{s_i}$ and set $V(\\theta):=\\sum_i\\theta_iS(s_i)$. Suppose a coordinate assignment $\\chi$ on these compounds satisfies $\\chi(M_\\theta)=\\sum_i\\theta_iq_i$ for fixed $q_i\\in\\mathbb R^d$. For each $x$, define the value to be $+\\infty$ if no $\\theta\\in\\Delta_m$ has coordinate $x$. Then the minimum represented value among those finite mixtures, with that empty-feasible-set convention, is exactly the finite-mixture envelope $f_{\\rm mix}(x)$ of [[thm-tdmath-finite-mixture-envelope]], with all its attainment, convexity, lower-semicontinuity, biconjugation, and supporting-face conclusions. This is the complete conditional transfer under the representation conclusion and affine coordinate assignment; the supplier definition and mass-one fact are provided locally.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 By [A1] and [L1], each $a_i=S(s_i)$ is finite. Under [A2], each $e_{s_i}$ is mass one; [L2] gives $m(M_\\theta)=\\sum_i\\theta_i m(e_{s_i})=\\sum_i\\theta_i=1$. The theorem's displayed compound sum assigns $M_\\theta$ the value $V(\\theta)=\\sum_i\\theta_i a_i$. By [A3], its coordinate is $\\sum_i\\theta_i q_i$. Hence mixtures in this fixed family with coordinate $x$ correspond exactly to $\\theta\\in\\Theta_x$ from [L3]. [A1, A2, A3, L1, L2, L3, algebra]\n\n2.1 The minimum of $V(\\theta)$ over those mixtures is therefore $\\min_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_x}\\sum_i\\theta_i a_i=f_{\\rm mix}(x)$ by [L3], with $+\\infty$ when $\\Theta_x$ is empty. Every further conclusion in the Statement follows by applying the corresponding conclusion of [L3] to the same finite data. This proof is conditional on [A1]–[A3] and does not supply the missing point-mass definition or repair the draft supplier. [A1, A3, L3, step 1.1] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "generation": {
          "role": "direct-corollary"
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-lever-rule",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "The two-point lever rule on a scalar interval",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "def-interval"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $v_a,v_b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $v_a<v_b$. A fraction $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ satisfies $v=(1-\\lambda)v_a+\\lambda v_b$ exactly when $v\\in[v_a,v_b]$ and\n\n$$\\lambda=\\frac{v-v_a}{v_b-v_a}.$$\n\nThis fraction is unique. At $v=v_a$ it is $0$, and at $v=v_b$ it is $1$; no admissible fraction gives a value outside the endpoint interval.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 From $v=(1-\\lambda)v_a+\\lambda v_b$ one obtains $v-v_a=\\lambda(v_b-v_a)$. Since $v_b-v_a>0$ and $0\\le\\lambda\\le1$, this gives $v_a\\le v\\le v_b$ and forces $\\lambda=(v-v_a)/(v_b-v_a)$. [F1, F2, given, algebra]\n\n2.1 Conversely, if $v_a\\le v\\le v_b$, the quotient $\\lambda=(v-v_a)/(v_b-v_a)$ lies in $[0,1]$ and substitution gives $v=(1-\\lambda)v_a+\\lambda v_b$. The same forced quotient proves uniqueness and yields the two endpoint values. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §1",
              "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
            },
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §1.1",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-concave-affine-maximizer-and-hessian",
        "reference": "items/lem-tdmath-concave-affine-maximizer-and-hessian.md",
        "design_state": "Owner escalation prerequisite authored; draft with independent pipeline review pending.",
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Concave supporting bound, affine maxima, and Hessian sign",
        "deps": [
          "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability",
          "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
          "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-mean-value-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, be open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be differentiable. For $x,y\\in U$, suppose the whole segment $[x,y]$ lies in $U$ and its concavity inequality holds:\n\n$$f(x+t(y-x))\\ge(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)\\qquad(0\\le t\\le1).$$\n\nThen the supporting bound is $f(y)\\le f(x)+Df(x)(y-x)$. In particular this bound holds for every $x,y$ in any convex subset of $U$ on which $f$ is concave. No global convexity of $U$ is assumed.\n\nLet $A:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^k$ be linear, $b\\in\\mathbb R^k$, and $F=U\\cap\\{x:Ax=b\\}$. Suppose $x_*\\in F$, $Df(x_*)h=0$ for every $h\\in\\ker A$, and for every $y\\in F$ the segment $[x_*,y]$ lies in $U$ and has the displayed concavity inequality. Then $x_*$ is a global maximum of $f$ on $F$. If $F$ is convex and $f|_F$ is strictly concave, meaning the inequality is strict for distinct endpoints and $0<t<1$, the maximum is unique.\n\nFor the curvature clause, suppose $f\\in C^2(U)$ and fix $x\\in U$. If, for every $h\\in\\mathbb R^d$, all sufficiently small $|t|$ satisfy $x\\pm th\\in U$ and\n\n$$f(x+th)+f(x-th)\\le2f(x),$$\n\nthen the Hessian bilinear form $D^2f(x)=D(Df)(x)$ satisfies $D^2f(x)[h,h]\\le0$ for every $h$. This condition holds whenever $f$ is concave on a neighborhood of $x$. Thus a $C^2$ function concave on an open convex region has negative-semidefinite Hessian there. Concavity alone does not assert strict curvature.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §1.5",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-constrained-curvature",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Second-order curvature tests on an affine feasible slice",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
