        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open and convex, let $A:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^k$ be linear, let $b\\in\\mathbb R^k$, and let $x_*\\in U$ satisfy $Ax_*=b$. Put $T=\\ker A$. Assume $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ is differentiable and has second directional derivatives represented by a continuous matrix field: there is a continuous map $H:U\\to\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ with symmetric values, and for every $x\\in U$ and $h\\in\\mathbb R^d$,\n\n$$\\left.\\frac{d^2}{dt^2}f(x+th)\\right|_{t=0}=Q_x(h):=h^{\\mathsf T}H(x)h.$$\n\nAssume stationarity $Df(x_*)h=0$ for every $h\\in T$. If $Q_{x_*}(h_0)<0$ for some $h_0\\in T$, then $x_*$ is not a local minimum of $f$ on $\\{x\\in U:Ax=b\\}$. If $Q_{x_*}(h)>0$ for every nonzero $h\\in T$, then $x_*$ is a strict local minimum on that feasible slice. If $T=\\{0\\}$, the latter conclusion holds because $x_*$ is the only feasible point.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Suppose $h_0\\in T$ and $Q_{x_*}(h_0)<0$. Set $\\phi(t)=f(x_*+th_0)$ for $t$ near $0$. Openness of $U$ permits this definition; stationarity gives $\\phi'(0)=0$. The identity in the Statement gives $\\phi''(t)=Q_{x_*+th_0}(h_0)$ wherever defined, and continuity of the entries of $H$ makes this negative for every sufficiently small $t\\ge0$. [given, L1, algebra]\n\n1.2 Suppose $T\\ne\\{0\\}$ and $Q_{x_*}(h)>0$ for each nonzero $h\\in T$. The unit sphere $S_T=\\{h\\in T:\\|h\\|_2=1\\}$ is nonempty, closed by [L2] and continuity of the norm, and bounded; [L3] makes it compact. The quadratic polynomial $h\\mapsto h^{\\mathsf T}H(x_*)h$ is continuous, so [L4] gives $\\lambda=\\min_{h\\in S_T}Q_{x_*}(h)>0$. [L1, L2, L3, L4, given]\n\n2.1 Choose such a small $t>0$. Applying [L5] to $\\phi'$ on $[0,t]$ gives $\\phi'(t)-\\phi'(0)=t\\phi''(s)<0$ for some $s\\in(0,t)$, hence $\\phi'(t)<0$; the same argument applies to every positive endpoint up to $t$. Applying [L5] to $\\phi$ on $[0,t]$ gives $\\phi(t)-\\phi(0)=t\\phi'(r)<0$ for some $r\\in(0,t)$. Since $h_0\\in\\ker A$, $x_*+th_0$ is feasible, so $x_*$ is not a local minimum. [L5, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 By continuity of the finitely many entries of $H$, choose $\\delta>0$ so that $\\|x-x_*\\|_2<\\delta$ implies $|Q_x(h)-Q_{x_*}(h)|<\\lambda/2$ for every unit vector $h$: expand the difference as a finite sum of entry differences times $h_i h_j$, each with $|h_i h_j|\\le1$. If $y\\ne x_*$ is feasible and $\\|y-x_*\\|_2<\\delta$, put $v=y-x_*$. Then $v\\in T$, and convexity of $U$ keeps $x_*+tv$ in $U$ for $0\\le t\\le1$. For $\\phi(t)=f(x_*+tv)$, stationarity gives $\\phi'(0)=0$, while homogeneity of the quadratic form gives $\\phi''(t)=Q_{x_*+tv}(v)\\ge(\\lambda/2)\\|v\\|_2^2>0$. [F1, L1, step 1.2, given, algebra]\n\n3.1 For any $t\\in(0,1]$, [L5] applied to $\\phi'$ on $[0,t]$ yields $\\phi'(t)-\\phi'(0)=t\\phi''(s)>0$ for some $s\\in(0,t)$, so $\\phi'(t)>0$. Applying [L5] to $\\phi$ on $[0,1]$ gives $\\phi(1)-\\phi(0)=\\phi'(r)>0$ for some $r\\in(0,1)$. Thus every distinct feasible $y$ with $\\|y-x_*\\|_2<\\delta$ has $f(y)>f(x_*)$. If $T=\\{0\\}$, $Ay=Ax_*$ implies $y-x_*\\in T=\\{0\\}$, so $x_*$ is the only feasible point. [L5, step 2.2, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "P. Bianchi, O. Fercoq, and W. Hachem, Elements of Convex Analysis and Optimization, §1.5",
              "url": "https://bianchi.wp.imt.fr/files/2023/11/m2ds.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 18.02SC, Session 30: Second Derivative Test",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-02sc-multivariable-calculus-fall-2010/a4685876e286873102a9937e1998a098_MIT18_02SC_MNotes_sd.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Bindel, Optimization Theory, §6.1.4, Numerical Methods for Data Science lecture notes",
              "url": "https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~bindel/nmds/00-Background/05-Optimization.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "The feasible tangent cone for nonnegative stoichiometric amounts",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
          "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $s,r\\ge1$, let $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^{s\\times r}$ be a stoichiometric matrix (column $k$ gives the signed species-amount change per unit extent of reaction $k$), and let $n^0\\in[0,\\infty)^s$ be initial species amounts. Extents $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r$ are measured in one fixed amount unit, so $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi$ has species amounts in that same unit; $\\nu$ is dimensionless. The feasible extent set is the polyhedron\n\n$$\\mathcal F=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r:n^0+\\nu\\xi\\in[0,\\infty)^s\\}.$$\n\nAt $\\xi_*\\in\\mathcal F$, let $J=\\{j:n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j=0\\}$ be the active species set. Define the Bouligand tangent cone $T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ to consist of all $h\\in\\mathbb R^r$ for which there are $t_k>0$ with $t_k\\to0$ and $h_k\\to h$ such that $\\xi_*+t_kh_k\\in\\mathcal F$. Then\n\n$$T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R^r:(\\nu h)_j\\ge0\\text{ for every }j\\in J\\}.$$\n\nIf $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ is open with $\\xi_*\\in U$, and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ is differentiable with a local minimum at $\\xi_*$ relative to $\\mathcal F\\cap U$, then $Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$ for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$. Conversely, if $U$ is convex and $f$ is convex and differentiable on $U$, this directional inequality for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ implies $f(\\xi)\\ge f(\\xi_*)$ for every $\\xi\\in\\mathcal F\\cap U$. Suppose $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^s$ is open, $n_*:=n^0+\\nu\\xi_*\\in V$, $\\Phi:V\\to\\mathbb R$ is totally differentiable at $n_*$, and $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n^0+\\nu\\xi)$ near $\\xi_*$. For each $j$, let $e_j\\in\\mathbb R^s$ have coordinate $1$ in position $j$ and $0$ in every other position; define $\\mu_j:=D\\Phi(n_*)e_j$. Then\n\n$$Df(\\xi_*)h=\\sum_{j=1}^s\\mu_j(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu)\\cdot h.$$\n\nThe tangent-cone and optimality claims are mathematical deductions. They do not assert that a particular physical system obeys this model or is at equilibrium.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Suppose $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$, and choose sequences $t_k,h_k$ from its definition. For each active $j\\in J$, feasibility gives $0+t_k(\\nu h_k)_j\\ge0$, hence $(\\nu h_k)_j\\ge0$. Since [F5] makes the finite matrix map continuous, $h_k\\to h$ implies $\\nu h_k\\to\\nu h$, so $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$. This proves one inclusion. [F5, given, algebra]\n\n1.2 Conversely suppose $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$ for all $j\\in J$. For each inactive $j$, put $b_j=n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j>0$. If $(\\nu h)_j<0$, restrict $0\\le t\\le b_j/[-(\\nu h)_j]$; if $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$, no restriction is needed. There are finitely many species, so choose a common $\\epsilon>0$ no larger than all positive bounds (or $\\epsilon=1$ if there are none). For $j\\in J$, $n^0_j+(\\nu(\\xi_*+th))_j=t(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$; for inactive $j$, the choice of $\\epsilon$ gives $b_j+t(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$ for $0\\le t\\le\\epsilon$. Thus $\\xi_*+th\\in\\mathcal F$ throughout that interval. Taking $h_k=h$ and any positive $t_k\\le\\epsilon$ tending to zero proves $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$. [given, choose, algebra]\n\n2.1 By steps 1.1–1.2, every tangent $h$ has a feasible ray $\\xi_*+th$ for all sufficiently small $t\\ge0$. Openness of $U$ keeps the initial part of this ray in $U$. At a relative local minimum, $[f(\\xi_*+th)-f(\\xi_*)]/t\\ge0$ for all sufficiently small $t>0$; differentiability [F1] makes its limit $Df(\\xi_*)h$. Hence $Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$. [F1, step 1.1, step 1.2, given]\n\n2.2 Now assume $U$ is convex and $f$ convex and differentiable. For any $\\xi\\in\\mathcal F\\cap U$, put $h=\\xi-\\xi_*$. For active $j$, $(\\nu h)_j=n_j(\\xi)-n_j(\\xi_*)=n_j(\\xi)\\ge0$, so step 1.2 makes $h$ a tangent direction; the segment $\\xi_*+th$ also lies in $\\mathcal F\\cap U$ by [F2]. Let $g(t)=f(\\xi_*+th)$. [F4] gives $(g(t)-g(0))/t\\le g(1)-g(0)$ for $0<t<1$; as $t\\downarrow0$, the left side tends to $Df(\\xi_*)h$ by [F1]. If every tangent derivative is nonnegative, then $f(\\xi)-f(\\xi_*)=g(1)-g(0)\\ge Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.2, given, algebra]\n\n3.1 For $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n^0+\\nu\\xi)$, the affine map has derivative $h\\mapsto\\nu h$. By [F3], $Df(\\xi_*)h=D\\Phi(n^0+\\nu\\xi_*)(\\nu h)$. Writing the total derivative in coordinates gives $\\sum_j(\\partial\\Phi/\\partial n_j)(\\nu h)_j=\\sum_j\\mu_j(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu)\\cdot h$, as claimed. [F3, given, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Bindel, Optimization Theory, §6.1.4, Numerical Methods for Data Science lecture notes",
              "url": "https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~bindel/nmds/00-Background/05-Optimization.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "IIT Madras EE5121, Constrained Optimization, Geometric tools and first-order necessary conditions",
              "url": "https://ee.iitm.ac.in/~uday/notes/opt/co1.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Local graph form at a nonsingular derivative minor",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map",
          "def-metric-topology",
          "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem",
          "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here “local graph dimension $k$” means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** reduction.\n\n1.1 First suppose $q<n$. Choose the coordinate permutation that puts the columns of the stated invertible minor into the second block $v\\in\\mathbb R^q$, and write the remaining coordinates as $u\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}$. Composing $F$ with the inverse permutation preserves openness and the $C^1$ property by [F1], [L3], and [L4]; its second-block derivative at $(u_*,v_*)$ is exactly the chosen invertible minor in the sense of [F2]. Since $n-q\\ge1$ and $q\\ge1$, [L1] applies and gives the neighborhoods, unique graph $\\varphi$, equivalence, and displayed derivative formula. [F1, F2, L1, L3, L4, given, construct]\n\n2.1 The graph map $u\\mapsto(u,\\varphi(u))$ is one-to-one because its first coordinate is $u$, and its inverse on the graph is coordinate projection. Its parameter domain $P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^{n-q}$, so this is the claimed local graph dimension. [step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Now suppose $q=n$. The selected minor is the whole square derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$, so it is invertible by [F2]. Apply [L2] on neighborhoods $V$ of $x_*$ and $W$ of $0$. Injectivity of $F|_V$ and $F(x_*)=0$ imply $F(x)=0$ for $x\\in V$ exactly when $x=x_*$. Thus the local zero set is a singleton and has the stated zero-dimensional graph form. [F2, L2, given, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Faye Jackson, MATH 395 notes, Implicit Function Theorem, pp. 99–106 and 127–132",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~alephnil/notes/MATH-395-notes.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "K. C. Border, Notes on the Implicit Function Theorem, Theorems 3–5",
              "url": "https://kcborder.caltech.edu/Courses/Notes/IFT.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis II, Theorem 8.5.6",
              "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_svinvfuncthm.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "reduction"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Regular rank form of the finite-component phase dimension count",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives",
          "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $C\\ge1$ be the number of components and $P\\ge1$ the number of phases. Use common positive real coordinates $T,p$, expressed in fixed units if they are given a physical interpretation, and, for each phase $\\alpha$, an interior composition vector\n\n$$x^\\alpha\\in\\Omega_C:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},$$\n\nwith the convention $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $\\Omega_1=\\{0\\}$. The final component fraction is $1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$. The ambient coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times\\Omega_C^P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^n$, where $n=2+P(C-1)$. For each phase $\\alpha$ and component $i$, let $\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)$ be a real-valued $C^1$ coordinate function; these are mathematical maps and no thermodynamic potential or physical realization is assumed.\n\nFor $P\\ge2$, define the $q=C(P-1)$ coexistence equations\n\n$$F_{i,\\alpha}(T,p,x^1,\\ldots,x^P)=\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)-\\mu_i^1(T,p,x^1)=0\\quad(1\\le i\\le C,\\ 2\\le\\alpha\\le P).$$\n\nAt a solution $z_*\\in U$, assume some $q\\times q$ derivative minor of $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is invertible. Then the local solution set is a graph of dimension\n\n$$n-q=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)=C-P+2.$$\n\nFor $P=1$ there are no coexistence equations and the coordinate domain itself has dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2$. In a specified reactive variant with $R\\ge1$, let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent dimensionless stoichiometric vectors and add the $R$ affinity equations $A_r=\\sum_{i=1}^C\\nu_i^r\\mu_i^1=0$. If the combined equation map has an invertible $(q+R)\\times(q+R)$ derivative minor at a solution and $q+R\\le n$, its local zero set has dimension\n\n$$n-q-R=C-P+2-R.$$\n\nThese are conditional local dimensions under the displayed nonsingular-minor assumptions. No dimension formula follows at singular solutions, on composition boundaries, or from equation counting alone; no empirical or general physical phase rule is asserted.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 For $P\\ge2$, $F$ has one scalar component for each pair $(i,\\alpha)$ with $1\\le i\\le C$ and $2\\le\\alpha\\le P$, so it has $q=C(P-1)$ components. Each is a difference of $C^1$ maps and therefore $F$ is $C^1$ on the open set $U$ by [F1, L2]. The assumed minor lets [L1] apply, giving local graph dimension $n-q$. Expanding $n=2+P(C-1)$ gives $n-q=2+PC-P-CP+C=C-P+2$. [F1, L1, L2, given, algebra]\n\n1.2 If $P=1$, no phase-equality equations are indexed because there is no $\\alpha\\ge2$. The local solution set is all of the open domain $U$, which has $n=2+(C-1)=C+1=C-P+2$ coordinates. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 In the reactive variant, each added affinity is a finite linear combination of $C^1$ coordinate functions and is therefore $C^1$ by [F1, L2]; the combined map has $q+R$ scalar equations. Its stated minor satisfies [L1], so the graph dimension is $n-q-R=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)-R=C-P+2-R$. Linear independence of the $\\nu^r$ identifies $R$ distinct reaction directions, while the full-rank minor remains a separate necessary assumption on the equation derivatives. [F1, L1, L2, given, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Faye Jackson, MATH 395 notes, Implicit Function Theorem, pp. 99–106 and 127–132",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~alephnil/notes/MATH-395-notes.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "K. C. Border, Notes on the Implicit Function Theorem, Theorems 3–5",
              "url": "https://kcborder.caltech.edu/Courses/Notes/IFT.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis II, Theorem 8.5.6",
              "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_svinvfuncthm.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
