    "items": [
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-vertical-separation-biconjugate",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A vertical separator and biconjugation outside the domain",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
          "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
          "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\{0,+\\infty\\}$ be the indicator of $[0,\\infty)$: $f(y)=0$ for $y\\ge0$ and $f(y)=+\\infty$ for $y<0$. At $z=(-1,0)$, the epigraph is separated by a vertical hyperplane (one whose coefficient of the epigraph-height coordinate is zero). The conjugate is $f^*(p)=0$ for $p\\le0$ and $+\\infty$ for $p>0$, so $f^{**}(-1)=+\\infty=f(-1)$. The affine minorants $\\ell_k(y)=-ky$, $k\\ge0$, display the unbounded dual values at $y=-1$.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct calculation.\n\n1.1 The domain is $[0,\\infty)$, so it is nonempty and $f$ is proper. Its epigraph is $E=\\{(y,s):y\\ge0,\\ s\\ge0\\}$, a nonempty closed convex set; [F3] therefore makes $f$ closed and convex. The point $(-1,0)$ lies outside $E$. With $u=(-1,0)$ and $c=0$, every $(y,s)\\in E$ satisfies $\\langle u,(y,s)\\rangle=-y\\le0<1=\\langle u,(-1,0)\\rangle$, so this is the strict vertical separator; its height coefficient is zero. [F1, F3, L1, given, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $p\\le0$ and $y\\ge0$, $py\\le0$, with equality at $y=0$, so $f^*(p)=0$. For $p>0$, given any real $M$ choose $y>\\max\\{0,M/p\\}$; then $py>M$, so the defining supremum is $+\\infty$. Therefore $\\operatorname{dom}f^*=(-\\infty,0]$. [F2, given, algebra, choose]\n\n2.1 At $x=-1$, the biconjugate is $\\sup_{p\\le0}(-p-f^*(p))=\\sup_{p\\le0}(-p)=+\\infty$: for every real bound $M$, the admissible choice $p=-\\max\\{M,0\\}-1$ gives $-p>M$. Thus $f^{**}(-1)=f(-1)$. [F2, step 1.2, algebra, choose]\n\n3.1 For each $k\\ge0$ and $y\\ge0$, $\\ell_k(y)=-ky\\le0=f(y)$, so $\\ell_k$ is an affine minorant. Its value at $-1$ is $k$, and the conjugate choice $p=-k$ has $f^*(-k)=0$, so the same family realizes arbitrarily large dual values at the point outside the domain. The conclusion is consistent with the full equality in [L2]. [F2, L2, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
