        "id": "ex-tdmath-singular-phase-rank",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A singular two-phase equation map with a four-dimensional solution set",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Take $C=2$ components and $P=2$ phases on the open coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times(0,1)^2\\subset\\mathbb R^4$. Set all four chemical-potential coordinate functions $\\mu_i^\\alpha$ identically equal to zero. The two coexistence equations then vanish identically, so the solution set is all of $U$ and has local dimension four. The regular formula would give $C-P+2=2$, but its nonsingular-minor hypothesis fails because the equation Jacobian is zero.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct calculation.\n\n1.1 For $C=P=2$, there are $n=2+2(2-1)=4$ coordinates and $q=2(2-1)=2$ equations. Since each $\\mu_i^\\alpha$ is identically zero, both differences $\\mu_i^2-\\mu_i^1$ equal zero at every point of $U$. Thus the zero set is exactly the open set $U$, which is locally parameterized by its four original coordinates. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 The derivative of each identically zero equation is the zero covector, so the equation Jacobian has rank zero and no $2\\times2$ minor is invertible. Therefore [L1] does not apply; the example does not contradict its regular dimension count, and it shows why equation count alone does not determine local dimension. [L1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
