      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-water-reaction-boundary-tangent",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Water formation at a boundary of the feasible extent interval",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "For the reaction $2\\,\\mathrm{H_2}+\\mathrm{O_2}\\to2\\,\\mathrm{H_2O}$, let extent $\\xi$ and species amounts use moles. The dimensionless stoichiometric column is $\\nu=(-2,-1,2)^{\\mathsf T}$ in the species order $(\\mathrm{H_2},\\mathrm{O_2},\\mathrm{H_2O})$, and take $n^0=(2,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}$. Then the feasible extent interval is $[0,1]$, and at $\\xi=0$ the tangent cone permits only forward directions $h\\ge0$. The matrix of hydrogen and oxygen atom counts annihilates $\\nu$. For the explicitly mathematical objective $\\Phi(n)=n_{\\mathrm{H_2O}}$, the induced extent function is $f(\\xi)=2\\xi$ and has a boundary minimum at zero. This is a stoichiometric calculation, not a claim about chemical equilibrium or a measured system.",
        "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct calculation.\n\n1.1 The species-amount vector is $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi=(2-2\\xi,1-\\xi,2\\xi)^{\\mathsf T}$. Nonnegativity of its third component requires $\\xi\\ge0$, and nonnegativity of its first two components requires $\\xi\\le1$; hence the feasible set is exactly $[0,1]$. [given, algebra]\n\n1.2 The elemental count matrix is $D=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0&2\\\\0&2&1\\end{pmatrix}$, with first row counting hydrogen atoms and second row oxygen atoms per molecule. Direct multiplication gives $D\\nu=(-4+4,-2+2)^{\\mathsf T}=(0,0)^{\\mathsf T}$, so every change $n(\\xi)-n^0=\\nu\\xi$ preserves both atom totals. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 At $\\xi_*=0$, only the water component is zero. Its directional change is $(\\nu h)_3=2h$, so [L1] gives $T_{\\mathcal F}(0)=\\{h:2h\\ge0\\}=[0,\\infty)$. A negative direction is infeasible because it would make the water amount $2\\xi$ negative. [L1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For $\\Phi(n)=n_3$, the induced function is $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n(\\xi))=2\\xi$. Its derivative at $0$ is $Df(0)h=2h\\ge0$ for every feasible tangent $h\\ge0$, and $f(\\xi)\\ge f(0)$ for every $\\xi\\in[0,1]$. This verifies the boundary first-order condition and minimum directly for this chosen mathematical objective. [L1, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
